# Optics 01 — Single-slit diffraction
#
# Light from a He–Ne laser (λ = 633 nm) passes through a slit of width
# a = 0.100 mm and falls on a screen L = 2.00 m away. The intensity is
#
#     I(θ) = I₀ (sin β / β)²,   β = π a sin θ / λ.
#
# (a) Where on the screen are the first and second dark fringes (a sin θ = m λ)?
#     How wide is the central bright fringe?
# (b) Find the first secondary maximum where dI/dθ = 0 between the first two
#     minima, and its intensity relative to I₀. (Textbook: β = 4.493, I/I₀ = 0.0472.)
# (c) What is the intensity 2.00 cm from the centre of the screen?
# (Halliday/Resnick ch. 36, Hecht §10.2)

λ = 633 nm
a = 0.100 mm
L = 2.00 m

# (a) dark fringes
θ1 = asin(λ / a)
θ2 = asin(2λ / a)
y1 = L tan(θ1)
y2 = L tan(θ2)
print "first dark fringe at y  =", y1 in mm to 8 digits
print "second dark fringe at y =", y2 in mm to 8 digits
print "central fringe width    =", 2 y1 in mm to 8 digits

# (b) the first side maximum, as a fraction of I₀
β(θ) = π a sin(θ) / λ
I(θ) = (sin(β(θ)) / β(θ))²
dI = I'      # naming the derivative: `solve I'(θ) = 0` is taken for a differential equation
solve dI(θ_max) = 0 for θ_max from 1.01 θ1 to 0.99 θ2
print "secondary maximum at β =", β(θ_max) to 8 digits
print "I/I0 there              =", I(θ_max) to 8 digits

# (c) on the screen, 2 cm from the centre
θ_c = atan((2.00 cm) / L)
print "I/I0 at y = 2.00 cm     =", I(θ_c) to 8 digits
